  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Given that $n\\ge -3$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+6)!}{(n+5)\\text{\u00a0}(n+3)!}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(n+6)!=(n+6)(n+5)(n+4)(n+3)!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n+5)$ and $(n+3)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+6)!}{(n+5)\\text{\u00a0}(n+3)!} & =\\frac{(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot (n+3)!}{(n+5)\\cdot (n+3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+6)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+5)}\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+3)!}}{\\cancel{(n+5)}\\cdot \\cancel{(n+3)!}} \\\\  & =(n+6)\\cdot (n+4) \\\\  & ={n}^{2}+10n+24\\end{aligned}\n$$",
